BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (Menuschool) Tj T* () Tj T* (Menuschool: Executive Summary) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Problem) Tj T* () Tj T* (Culinary students spend much of their training learning to cook, but far less time learning to) Tj T* (run the numbers behind a menu. Food cost, portion yield, and pricing are what decide whether a) Tj T* (restaurant survives, yet many programs teach these topics with handouts, spreadsheets, or) Tj T* (textbook examples that use outdated or made-up prices. Instructors we spoke with told us they) Tj T* (want to teach costing with real data, but building costed lesson plans by hand takes a long) Tj T* (time. A single recipe can have fifteen or twenty ingredients, each with different purchase) Tj T* (units, trim loss, and yields, and prices change often.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Allergen labeling is a second gap. Students are expected to know the major allergens and how to) Tj T* (communicate them on a menu, but instructors usually cover this in a separate lecture instead of) Tj T* (building it into every recipe students work with. As a result, costing and allergen awareness) Tj T* (are taught as isolated topics instead of as part of one menu planning process.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The result is that graduates often enter kitchens without practical experience in menu costing.) Tj T* (Several chefs we interviewed said this is one of the first things they have to teach new hires.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our Solution) Tj T* () Tj T* (Menuschool is a software platform and consulting service that converts instructors' recipes) Tj T* (into costed, allergen-labeled digital lesson plans. An instructor enters or uploads a recipe.) Tj T* (The software matches each ingredient to a pricing database, applies purchase unit conversions) Tj T* (and yield factors, calculates cost per portion and food-cost percentage, and flags the major) Tj T* (allergens. The instructor can then turn the recipe into an assignment, for example asking) Tj T* (students to reduce the food-cost percentage below a target without removing an allergen) Tj T* (warning, or to build a three-course menu within a budget.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Students see the same data the instructor sees and can change ingredients, portions, and menu) Tj T* (prices to watch the numbers move. The goal is for menu planning to feel like a real decision) Tj T* (with trade-offs, not a worksheet.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The consulting side supports programs that have large recipe collections and little time. We) Tj T* (convert existing recipes into the platform, set up pricing sources, and help instructors map) Tj T* (lesson plans to their course outcomes.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Market) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our initial customers are culinary programs in the United States, including community college) Tj T* (programs, private culinary schools, and some high school career and technical programs. Based) Tj T* (on our own count from public program listings, there are roughly several hundred such programs) Tj T* (nationally, though we have not yet verified this figure against an authoritative source. We) Tj T* (estimate each program has between three and ten instructors who teach some form of menu) Tj T* (planning or cost control.) Tj T* () Tj T* (If we assume an average program pays a few thousand dollars per year, the addressable market in) Tj T* (the United States is in the low single-digit millions of dollars annually. This is a small) Tj T* (market on its own, which is why we see it as a starting point. Later opportunities include) Tj T* (hospitality management programs, corporate training for restaurant groups, and continuing) Tj T* (education for working cooks.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Business Model) Tj T* () Tj T* (We plan three sources of revenue. First, annual program licenses priced per instructor seat,) Tj T* (which we expect to be our main recurring revenue. Second, one-time conversion and onboarding) Tj T* (services, where we digitize a program's recipe library for a fixed fee. Third, optional) Tj ET
endstream
